From 91745cb9b71ee739cb106c92878b320650d97536 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 14:23:03 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 13/44] feat(chexc): effect requirement checking, 'performs'
 clause (proposal 8.2)

'perform c' requires a handler capability for the command's type, exactly as
'throw e' requires one for the exception's; a 'handle' clause supplies one per
command type it lists, over the region a 'catch' covers; and a 'performs' clause
declares the requirement, preceding 'throws' (proposal 9.1 rule 8).

Entries share FuncTy::capTys with 'throws' rather than getting a parallel list.
This is sound because the roots are disjoint -- an effect entry is a Command
subtype, an exception entry an Exception subtype -- and discharge is by
subtyping, so a catch can never discharge a perform nor a handle a throw. That
disjointness is what carries interning, instantiation, functional-type subtyping
and cjo serialization for effects with no further change (decision D17). Under
evidence passing (8.2) effect capabilities become real runtime values while
exception ones stay erased, and at that point the lists MUST split; recorded.

The 9.1 policy differences are enforced where they bite:
- effect requirements are NEVER inferred. Command-typed residuals are excluded
  from collection, so an undeclared 'perform' is reported rather than silently
  added to the declaration -- where an exception in the same position would be
  inferred. IsCommandTy compares DECLARATIONS through the supertype set, not
  IsSubtype against the uninstantiated 'Command<T>', which never matches a
  concrete 'C <: Command<Int64>'; getting this wrong reopened exactly the
  inference hole the policy forbids, and the internal-function negative test is
  what caught it.
- 'main' cannot declare 'performs': there is no default effect handler.
- entries must be Command subtypes, mirroring the Exception rule for 'throws'.

Also fixes a pre-existing hole this work exposed (ledger E2): the try block of a
try/handle was never capability-checked at all. With handlers present the parser
rebuilds the block as a lambda and sema types THAT copy, leaving the original
untyped; since every demand is guarded by Ty::IsTyCorrect, walking the untyped
copy raised nothing -- dropping EXCEPTION demands too, not just effect ones. The
pass now walks whichever copy carries the types, and treats handler bodies like
catch bodies, outside the try's own scope.

Tests: compiler/CheckedExceptions/performs, 4 cases -- handler round trip, the
undeclared-perform error, non-discharge across both capability kinds in both
directions, and Command-subtype validation. CheckedExceptions 74/74; ctest 49/49.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
